    "AI ethics is the branch of ethics that examines the moral issues and responsibilities surrounding the design, development, deployment, and use of artificial intelligence systems in society.",
    "Bias in AI arises when training data reflects historical prejudices or societal inequalities — for example, a hiring algorithm trained on biased past data may discriminate against certain genders or ethnicities.",
    "Algorithmic bias can also be introduced through feature selection, labelling choices, and model design decisions, meaning bias is not solely a data problem but a systemic one across the AI pipeline.",
    "Fairness in AI means ensuring that AI systems treat all individuals and groups equitably, without favouring or disadvantaging people based on race, gender, age, socioeconomic status, or other protected characteristics.",
    "Accountability requires that developers, organisations, and deployers of AI systems take responsibility for the outcomes of their systems — including unintended harms, errors, and negative consequences.",
